# Target C-II — corrected joint-feasibility O--O path/hub/strip extends through `t<=1.8615`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only corrected high-`t` structural strengthening  
**Scope:** full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8615`  
**Dependencies:** `target_c_joint_feasible_opposite_branch_path_t1861_2026-09-15.md` and its corrected physical opposite-branch argument  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_joint_feasible_opposite_branch_path_t18615.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The corrected joint-feasibility architecture still has quantitative room above `1.861`.
At

\[
T={3723\over2000}=1.8615
\]

use the slightly lower terminal-energy threshold

\[
\lambda={4979\over10000}=0.4979.
\]

The same retained-mass feasibility calculation forces

\[
\boxed{z>2.0975-o(1).}
\tag{J615.1}
\]

The matched atomic floor gives

\[
\delta<0.090670+o(1),
\tag{J615.2}
\]

and weighted Markov then leaves

\[
\boxed{P_{low}>0.0030-o(1).}
\tag{J615.3}
\]

Thus one exact dominant phase contains more than

\[
\boxed{0.0010D-o(D)}
\tag{J615.4}

actual outer-to-outer trains with two-terminal directional energy at most `0.4979`.
The physical Section-157 bracing inequality still rules out the same-oriented terminal
branch, so these trains use opposite oriented terminal branches and connect the same
two disjoint boundary arcs.  Their complement dual is therefore a simple path.

The exact diameter-path Helly theorem supplies one common routing hub on that path.
Switching at every nonhub path boundary is one-sided toward the hub.  Since the total
actual-diameter interval endpoint budget is at most `2 pi D+o(D)`, one nonhub boundary
has at most

\[
\boxed{6283}
\tag{J615.5}

moving actual diameter rows.  Its exact nonzero strip reaction is generated by one or
two actual switching diameter bars.

No retracted projective-branch shortcut is used.

---

## 1. Retained-mass feasibility

Keep

\[
U={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-\kappa/2,
\qquad \kappa\ge0,
\]

and

\[
C_{76}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0076,
\qquad \Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\]

At `t<=T`, put

\[
U_0={9T\over2}-2\pi.
\]

The exact verifier gives

\[
U_0<2.093565,
\qquad
\delta\le{U_0-C_{76}\over2}<0.090670.
\tag{J615.6}
\]

Use the corrected parent feasibility function `G(delta,z)` and set

\[
H(z):=G(F(z),z),
\qquad F(z)=z-6\sin(z/6).
\]

Exactly as in the parent theorem, `G_delta<0` on the relevant rectangle, so replacing
`delta` by its lower bound `F(z)` is adverse.  The derivative proof also survives the
slightly enlarged rectangle.  The certificate checks

\[
5U_0^2<32\Delta_2,
\qquad
G_\delta>-7.55,
\qquad
G_z>{24\over25},
\qquad
F'(z)<{1\over16}
\]

for `0<=z<=2.0975`, and hence

\[
H'(z)>0.
\]

The outward rational/Taylor evaluation at the endpoint is

\[
\boxed{H(2.0975)<-3.36\times10^{-4}.}
\tag{J615.7}
\]

Therefore `z<=2.0975-o(1)` is infeasible, proving (J615.1).

---

## 2. Low-energy pair weight

Let `P_OO^dom` be retained dominant O--O pair weight.  The existing endpoint-count
inequality gives

\[
P_{OO}^{dom}\ge z-U-o(1).
\]

If `P_low` denotes the retained pair weight with `e_tau<=lambda`, weighted Markov gives

\[
P_{low}\ge P_{OO}^{dom}-{\delta\over\lambda}-o(1).
\]

Substitute the definition of `U`:

\[
P_{low}
\ge z-U_0+{\kappa\over2}
+\delta\left(2-{1\over\lambda}\right)-o(1).
\tag{J615.8}
\]

Because `lambda<1/2`, the coefficient of `delta` is negative.  Adversely set
`kappa=0`, use the upper bound in (J615.6), and insert `z>2.0975-o(1)`.  The exact
certificate obtains

\[
P_{low}>0.0031704-o(1)>0.0030-o(1).
\]

At most three exact projective phases occur, so one phase carries pair weight above

\[
0.0010568-o(1)>0.0010-o(1).
\]

Pair weight per complete train is at most one, proving (J615.4).

---

## 3. Physical opposite-branch margin

For a selected train, the audited terminal-energy theorem gives endpoint deviations
`alpha,beta` with

\[
e_\tau\ge(1-\cos\alpha)+(1-\cos\beta)-o(1).
\]

The parent theorem proves that a same-oriented terminal branch would satisfy

\[
{r_\tau^2\over D^2}
\le\lambda-{\lambda^2\over4}+o(1),
\]

whereas Section-157 bracing gives

\[
{r_\tau\over D}
\ge1-\delta-{\lambda\over2}-o(1).
\]

At the exact upper bound for `delta`, the companion certificate proves

\[
\boxed{
\left(1-\delta_{max}-{\lambda\over2}\right)^2
-\left(\lambda-{\lambda^2\over4}\right)
>1.78\times10^{-4}.
}
\tag{J615.9}
\]

Thus the same-oriented branch is impossible.  Every selected train has opposite
oriented terminal branches.  Since `lambda<1/2`, the two host arcs are separated by a
fixed positive gap, so the pairwise noncrossing same-phase crosscuts are totally
ordered and form one exact complement-dual path.

---

## 4. Hub and finite one-sided strip

Apply the existing diameter-path Helly theorem to the selected path.  Every actual
diameter support interval contains one common dual region `R_*`.  Therefore every
nonhub path boundary has one-sided switching toward `R_*`.

If `N_i` is the number of diameter intervals starting or stopping at an internal path
boundary, then

\[
\sum_iN_i\le2|H|\le2\pi D+o(D).
\]

After deleting the single hub boundary, there remain more than `0.0010D-o(D)` internal
boundaries.  Using `pi<355/113`,

\[
{2(355/113)\over0.0010}=6283.1858\ldots<6284.
\]

Hence some nonhub boundary has integer switch count at most `6283`, proving (J615.5).
The reaction-nondegeneracy and conic-Caratheodory theorems then reduce its exact layer
reaction to one or two actual switching diameter vectors.

---

## 5. Frontier effect

The corrected high-`t` structural interval is now

```text
1.844 < t <= 1.8615:
  retained dominant mass z > 2.0975;
  >0.0030 D low-energy dominant O-O pair weight;
  >0.0010 D same-phase O-O path trains;
  physical opposite oriented terminal branches;
  exact dual path + common diameter-routing hub;
  one-sided switching at every nonhub boundary;
  one nonhub strip with <=6283 moving diameter rows;
  exact nonzero reaction generated by <=2 actual bars.
```

A floating-point optimization of this same architecture puts its zero-path-mass limit
near `1.86180`.  The remaining decimal room is small.  Further progress should mainly
come from exploiting the one-sided finite strip or strengthening the retained-mass
input, not from treating the continuous root as a closing theorem.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only extension of the corrected joint-feasibility
path/hub/strip theorem through `t<=1.8615`.  C-II and Level I remain open.
